  "slug": "intratumoral-cell-c25b",
  "summary": "Twenty published items and two preprints link intratumoral myeloid, stromal and tumor-intrinsic programs to immune exclusion and anti-PD-1 response.",
  "synthesis": "Recent intratumoral single-cell and spatial work concentrates on myeloid and stromal compartments governing checkpoint response. Several studies implicate suppressive myeloid states: SRGN+ neutrophils drive CD8+ T-cell exhaustion in glioma, FABP4 stabilizes PD-L1 in immunosuppressive myeloid cells, and AhR blockade boosts M1 macrophages and anti-PD-1 efficacy. Stromal programs recur, including myofibroblastic CAFs linked to CD8+ T-cell exclusion in NSCLC and MGPhi CAFs tied to MDSC-mediated immunosuppression in melanoma. Tumor-intrinsic drivers include SMAD3-mediated cell state transitions in bladder cancer and a PTN+ glioblastoma subclone promoting anti-inflammatory TAM polarization. Therapeutic nominations include HK2 inhibition raising CCL5-dependent CD8+ infiltration, and an agent-based model suggesting Treg blockade. The signal rose by a ratio of 5.0 across 20 published items and 2 preprints, with no code or industry signals; these counts reflect activity volume and do not establish cause.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-26"
}
